<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>hr.hrg.jcodebuddy</groupId>
        <artifactId>jcodebuddy-parent</artifactId>
        <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>jcodebuddy-core</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>JCodeBuddy Core</name>
    <description>
        The metadata engine: the one source representation and the parse path over it, the metadata model,
        the class index with its content identity, and the queries a consumer asks of them — one engine for
        codegen, analysis, reporting and the watch loop (DEC-037).
    </description>

    <!--
        WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS, AND WHAT IT CARRIES NOW.

        This module is the engine: it reads sources through DEC-030's one representation, holds the metadata
        model, the class index (DEC-029) with its relations, the queries over them, and the freshness
        contract (DEC-037). Everything else in the reactor is a consumer or a transport, and a consumer that
        needs fresh metadata reads this one model rather than growing a path of its own.

        It was also the home of the generated-code marker vocabulary and its parser until step 3.0l moved
        them to `jcodebuddy-generated`, and that is why the move mattered. DEC-035's readers are linters,
        migration tools, IDE inspections and AI agents — not generators — and they should not resolve
        OpenRewrite and Jackson to learn where generated code stops. The leaf is a leaf so that stays true,
        and this module is free to be the engine without lending its dependencies to them.

        Since 2026-10-02 (DEC-037, plan step 3.0f) this module holds the engine, and that is why it declares
        OpenRewrite and Jackson: parsing goes through DEC-030's one representation, and the metadata
        documents are JSON.

        The direction of the dependency is still the honest one, and step 3.0f-2 was spent proving it: the
        engine names no consumer. Where it needed what a consumer had (a kind resolver, a diagnostic
        channel, a member shape, a JSON helper, the `.jcodebuddy` name), the engine defines the port or owns
        the vocabulary, and the consumer implements or delegates.
    -->

    <dependencies>
        <!-- The one source representation (DEC-030): read, query and write Java through the LST, never a
             second parser. `rewrite-java-25` is the JDK 25 dialect the gate compiles at. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-java</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-java-25</artifactId>
            <version>${openrewrite.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The metadata documents are JSON (`.jcodebuddy/index/classes.json`, DEC-029). Jackson 3 is what
             this reactor manages (`tools.jackson.core`, `${jackson.version}`), not the `com.fasterxml` line. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test-only, and the parser itself stayed pure JDK where it could. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
